My name is Pavan Ruppanguntla.

3
00:00:04,978 --> 00:00:09,528
Before I start the presentation, I want
to mention that the views expressed

4
00:00:09,530 --> 00:00:14,955
are personal and does not reflect my
current employer or previous employers.

5
00:00:15,899 --> 00:00:18,779
today I'm going to talk about a topic
that sits at the intersection of

6
00:00:18,830 --> 00:00:23,531
machine learning, credit risk, model
governance, and technology architecture.

7
00:00:24,345 --> 00:00:28,433
The feature flags for
safer ML credit decisions.

8
00:00:29,171 --> 00:00:34,195
Before I get into the feature flags
themselves, I want to briefly explain why

9
00:00:34,224 --> 00:00:38,546
this topic is important to me and how it
connects to my professional experience.

10
00:00:39,625 --> 00:00:43,370
I have more than seventeen years of
experience in financial services,

11
00:00:44,042 --> 00:00:48,211
with a career focused primarily on
consumer credit risk, credit cards,

12
00:00:48,275 --> 00:00:51,685
credit decisioning, analytics,
and model risk management.

13
00:00:52,687 --> 00:00:56,300
I have worked across organizations
including Bank of America and Barclays

14
00:00:56,775 --> 00:01:00,786
in roles spanning credit strategy,
quantitative analytics, and model

15
00:01:00,786 --> 00:01:03,507
validation and model risk management.

16
00:01:04,985 --> 00:01:09,851
My background combines the two areas that
don't always, naturally sit together.

17
00:01:10,447 --> 00:01:15,094
The first is credit business
strategy, understanding underwriting,

18
00:01:15,735 --> 00:01:20,039
credit line assignment, acquisition
strategies, portfolio performance,

19
00:01:20,145 --> 00:01:24,892
and how credit decisions affect both
customers and financial institutions.

20
00:01:25,729 --> 00:01:30,106
the second is, quantitative and model risk
management, which involves understanding

21
00:01:30,107 --> 00:01:34,286
how statistical and machine learning
models are developed, validated,

22
00:01:34,581 --> 00:01:36,598
monitored, challenged, and governed.

23
00:01:37,556 --> 00:01:40,975
In my work, I have been involved
with models supporting large-scale

24
00:01:40,987 --> 00:01:45,327
consumer credit decisions, including
underwriting and credit line strategies.

25
00:01:45,982 --> 00:01:50,255
I also worked with senior business
risk technology and governance

26
00:01:50,264 --> 00:01:54,744
stakeholders and participated
in regulatory and audit exams.

27
00:01:55,551 --> 00:01:58,791
So from my perspective, a model
isn't simply an algorithm.

28
00:01:59,868 --> 00:02:04,303
A model becomes part of a decision-making
system, and once that model is making

29
00:02:04,316 --> 00:02:09,440
real decisions about customers' credit
exposure and risk, the question becomes

30
00:02:09,471 --> 00:02:12,007
much bigger than, "Does the model work?"

31
00:02:12,966 --> 00:02:16,930
We also have to look into
and ask: Can we control it?

32
00:02:17,714 --> 00:02:19,111
Can we explain what happened?

33
00:02:19,358 --> 00:02:20,485
Can we observe it?

34
00:02:21,064 --> 00:02:27,709
And most importantly, if we see an
issue, can we safely stop and change it?

35
00:02:29,356 --> 00:02:32,284
That is the motivation
behind today's, presentation.

36
00:02:33,024 --> 00:02:37,164
Machine learning gives us increasingly
powerful tools for credit decisioning.

37
00:02:38,422 --> 00:02:43,134
But powerful models require equally
strong mechanisms for control,

38
00:02:43,445 --> 00:02:45,998
observability, governance, and rollback.

39
00:02:46,724 --> 00:02:49,931
And that's where feature flags
can play an important role.

40
00:02:50,787 --> 00:02:53,052
So with that background,
let's look at the problem.

41
00:02:54,251 --> 00:02:56,109
The ML deployment challenge.

42
00:02:57,393 --> 00:03:00,216
Traditional software and machine
learning models may look similar

43
00:03:00,219 --> 00:03:03,877
from a deployment perspective,
but they behave very differently.

44
00:03:04,572 --> 00:03:07,441
Traditional software generally
follows deterministic rules.

45
00:03:08,419 --> 00:03:11,438
If the inputs and codes are
the same, we generally expect

46
00:03:11,486 --> 00:03:12,551
the output to be the same.

47
00:03:13,508 --> 00:03:15,963
Machine learning models are
different, as you all know.

48
00:03:16,867 --> 00:03:21,549
They learn relationships from historical
data, and when that model encounters

49
00:03:21,641 --> 00:03:27,998
live production data, the environment
may not look exactly like the

50
00:03:27,999 --> 00:03:30,047
development or validation environment.

51
00:03:31,166 --> 00:03:36,368
customer behavior can change, economic
conditions can change, data distributions

52
00:03:36,377 --> 00:03:39,142
can change, features can change.

53
00:03:39,305 --> 00:03:43,492
And feature that was consistently
available during development may

54
00:03:43,498 --> 00:03:45,303
suddenly become missing or delayed.

55
00:03:46,184 --> 00:03:50,033
And relationships that were
stable historically may shift.

56
00:03:50,768 --> 00:03:53,067
The presentation
highlights this core risk.

57
00:03:53,708 --> 00:03:57,960
A model can perform very well
during validation and still behave

58
00:03:57,967 --> 00:04:03,639
unexpectedly in production because of
population shift, missing attributes,

59
00:04:03,699 --> 00:04:05,443
or evolving customer behavior.

60
00:04:06,775 --> 00:04:09,135
This is particularly
important into credit.

61
00:04:09,790 --> 00:04:12,770
Imagine that we have developed
a new underwriting model.

62
00:04:13,364 --> 00:04:17,183
We have completed the model development,
completed validation, testing

63
00:04:17,224 --> 00:04:22,288
performance, performance testing,
and, received the required approvals.

64
00:04:22,704 --> 00:04:23,662
The model is ready.

65
00:04:24,354 --> 00:04:26,964
Traditionally, we might
think of like, okay, the next

66
00:04:26,964 --> 00:04:28,157
step is deploying the model.

67
00:04:28,940 --> 00:04:30,922
But deployment creates
an important question.

68
00:04:31,470 --> 00:04:34,742
Does putting the model into
production automatically mean that

69
00:04:34,742 --> 00:04:37,549
we should immediately allow it
to make the customer decisions?

70
00:04:38,469 --> 00:04:42,496
I would argue that there are two different
events, and separating them is one of the

71
00:04:42,496 --> 00:04:44,839
most important ideas in this presentation.

72
00:04:45,831 --> 00:04:47,657
What is a feature flag?

73
00:04:48,677 --> 00:04:51,087
So what exactly is a feature flag?

74
00:04:52,954 --> 00:04:56,366
At a high level, a feature flag
is a runtime control layer between

75
00:04:56,381 --> 00:04:58,357
deployed functionality and activation.

76
00:04:59,198 --> 00:05:02,143
In credit decisioning, that means
a feature flag can determine

77
00:05:02,644 --> 00:05:06,863
which model, policy, or decision
path handles an eligible request

78
00:05:07,377 --> 00:05:09,020
without requiring a new software.

79
00:05:09,921 --> 00:05:13,128
Think as in three separate stages.

80
00:05:13,544 --> 00:05:18,863
First deploy, then you have
control, and then you activate.

81
00:05:19,596 --> 00:05:26,829
So this distinction is very, very, very
important because the technical act of

82
00:05:26,865 --> 00:05:29,798
putting something into production does
not necessarily mean that customers

83
00:05:29,803 --> 00:05:31,938
immediately experience the cha- change.

84
00:05:33,018 --> 00:05:36,763
The model can be present, the
infrastructure can be tested, the

85
00:05:36,765 --> 00:05:40,784
logging can be validated, and your
monitoring controls are there.

86
00:05:41,501 --> 00:05:44,837
And only after those checks are
complete, we allow the model to

87
00:05:44,837 --> 00:05:46,376
influence the customer decisions.

88
00:05:46,908 --> 00:05:49,760
That gives an additional control point.

89
00:05:50,669 --> 00:05:53,843
And in a regulated financial
environment, additional control

90
00:05:53,844 --> 00:05:55,991
points can be extremely valuable.

91
00:05:57,273 --> 00:06:03,261
So that's why we have to separate
deployment from activation So

92
00:06:03,456 --> 00:06:05,590
let's explore that a little more.

93
00:06:06,578 --> 00:06:08,781
The first stage is deployment.

94
00:06:09,412 --> 00:06:10,625
The model is in production.

95
00:06:10,946 --> 00:06:12,612
Infrastructure has been verified.

96
00:06:13,112 --> 00:06:14,396
Logging is confirmed.

97
00:06:14,995 --> 00:06:17,054
The inference pipeline is available.

98
00:06:17,986 --> 00:06:21,607
monitoring is connected, but the
model is not necessarily making

99
00:06:21,626 --> 00:06:23,227
live customer decisions yet.

100
00:06:24,131 --> 00:06:26,033
The second stage is activation.

101
00:06:26,138 --> 00:06:28,920
An approved configuration
change makes the model active.

102
00:06:29,496 --> 00:06:33,741
The important point is that we don't
necessarily need another software release

103
00:06:33,746 --> 00:06:35,689
simply to expand or reduce exposure.

104
00:06:36,433 --> 00:06:37,477
Why does this matter?

105
00:06:38,034 --> 00:06:41,869
Because it creates a clear boundary
between technical release management

106
00:06:42,677 --> 00:06:45,187
and business decision authority.

107
00:06:45,828 --> 00:06:48,366
Technology teams can verify
that the system works.

108
00:06:49,104 --> 00:06:53,715
Model risk systems can verify that the
model and model controls are appropriate,

109
00:06:54,491 --> 00:06:58,304
and business and risk stakeholders
can determine when the model should

110
00:06:58,311 --> 00:07:00,184
actually begin affecting the customers.

111
00:07:01,276 --> 00:07:04,277
so that gives us the opportunity
to test the production environment

112
00:07:04,313 --> 00:07:05,741
before even, customers are…

113
00:07:06,039 --> 00:07:07,439
outcomes are affected.

114
00:07:08,197 --> 00:07:12,303
We can veri-verify, whether the
features are actually available, they

115
00:07:12,304 --> 00:07:16,925
are arriving on time, there are any
downstream systems, receiving the expected

116
00:07:16,926 --> 00:07:22,638
information, the monitoring, dashboards
working, and, and, and are the expected

117
00:07:22,691 --> 00:07:24,643
decision distributions appearing.

118
00:07:25,871 --> 00:07:29,201
This is a very different mindset
from simply saying, "Okay," say,

119
00:07:29,318 --> 00:07:30,695
"The model passed validation.

120
00:07:30,695 --> 00:07:31,695
Now we can turn it on."

121
00:07:32,765 --> 00:07:37,018
Instead, we need to take a step
back and s-- and understand that

122
00:07:37,110 --> 00:07:38,913
the model passed the validation.

123
00:07:39,888 --> 00:07:44,253
Now, is it safe to introduce the
model into production environment?

124
00:07:44,671 --> 00:07:53,043
And that's where in my, in my experience,
progressive reloads have helped.

125
00:07:54,142 --> 00:08:00,331
Progressive re-reloads are, nothing
but rather than moving the model

126
00:08:00,909 --> 00:08:05,811
into production and running it at a
hundred percent, you slowly increase

127
00:08:05,811 --> 00:08:09,614
the throttle, say, from ten percent
to twenty percent to thirty, and

128
00:08:09,614 --> 00:08:11,260
then finally a hundred percent.

129
00:08:12,138 --> 00:08:17,528
So you're introducing the model to
the real-time environment gradually.

130
00:08:18,807 --> 00:08:24,252
So because of that, m-when we
are starting, we, we start with

131
00:08:24,339 --> 00:08:28,906
a small approved population, then
we observe the model, examine its

132
00:08:28,911 --> 00:08:35,122
behavior, we compare the results with
expectations, and only then we expand.

133
00:08:36,336 --> 00:08:41,331
So, this should not be an informal
experiment, where someone simply

134
00:08:41,332 --> 00:08:42,619
changes the configuration.

135
00:08:43,271 --> 00:08:49,489
but it, it should be more like, okay,
you have to get approval at each stage

136
00:08:50,063 --> 00:08:57,464
and make sure every aspect is working
fine, and then you are allowing the model

137
00:08:57,983 --> 00:09:01,785
to run at different throttle levels.

138
00:09:02,504 --> 00:09:05,609
So instead of simply saying,
"Turn it on," we define which

139
00:09:05,611 --> 00:09:07,505
customers our products are included.

140
00:09:08,127 --> 00:09:13,887
Say, we want to, say, the customers who
are current clients, just turn it on them

141
00:09:13,896 --> 00:09:17,564
first and then expand it to, non-clients.

142
00:09:18,308 --> 00:09:20,306
these characteristics
need to be documented.

143
00:09:20,439 --> 00:09:24,880
I was just giving a simple example, but
there can be more, more sort of segments

144
00:09:25,414 --> 00:09:31,257
m-based on, say, products or the type
of, customer behavior you, you have

145
00:09:31,258 --> 00:09:37,492
seen in past or whether, whether they
are coming from one of your referral

146
00:09:37,492 --> 00:09:40,220
channels or your own banking channel.

147
00:09:40,786 --> 00:09:42,040
So it, it again depends.

148
00:09:42,097 --> 00:09:47,296
I mean, first you want to experiment,
or observe, how the model is, acting.

149
00:09:47,900 --> 00:09:53,875
so to start, always starting with a, a
small set of population is always helpful.

150
00:09:54,210 --> 00:10:01,179
so there is, there is one more aspect
to that, which is, when, when you're

151
00:10:01,403 --> 00:10:06,221
looking at small population and
observing the model, you can, you can

152
00:10:06,221 --> 00:10:08,200
also look at that decision traceability.

153
00:10:08,993 --> 00:10:13,698
So for every decision in, in the-- for
that small segment, you can, you can

154
00:10:13,716 --> 00:10:20,066
see, okay, which version of the model
was active- cohort that the customer

155
00:10:20,092 --> 00:10:26,550
belonged to, and most importantly, why
did this customer got this decision?

156
00:10:27,760 --> 00:10:33,166
So you're tracing the outcome
of the model back to different

157
00:10:33,170 --> 00:10:39,040
attributes, different paths, and
coming up with a, an explanation.

158
00:10:40,417 --> 00:10:45,636
So, then when you're rolling out,
so at, at, at different throttle

159
00:10:45,636 --> 00:10:51,083
levels, you have, a champion model
and a, and a challenger model.

160
00:10:51,748 --> 00:10:55,849
champion is where, you're looking at
the, existing production model, and

161
00:10:55,849 --> 00:10:57,651
challenger is obviously the new model.

162
00:10:58,468 --> 00:11:02,707
So instead of immediately giving the
challenger, decision-making authority,

163
00:11:02,769 --> 00:11:04,621
we can initially run it in shadow mode.

164
00:11:05,414 --> 00:11:08,238
A challenger receives the
same relevant information.

165
00:11:08,261 --> 00:11:11,825
It generates an output, but that
output doesn't affect the customer.

166
00:11:12,319 --> 00:11:14,639
Instead, we record it,
compare it with the champion.

167
00:11:15,105 --> 00:11:18,209
This creates an important l-
important learning opportunity.

168
00:11:18,518 --> 00:11:21,874
We can look at where the models
disagree, which customer segments

169
00:11:21,888 --> 00:11:23,406
show the largest differences.

170
00:11:24,518 --> 00:11:26,932
Are the differences consistent
with what we expected?

171
00:11:27,455 --> 00:11:29,516
Are there changes in risk segmentation?

172
00:11:29,869 --> 00:11:34,072
So, so basically, we, we run this
challenger model in shadow mode.

173
00:11:35,044 --> 00:11:37,943
The advantage being it doesn't
affect the customer outcomes.

174
00:11:38,220 --> 00:11:42,243
you can validate the required
features, in, in a clear production

175
00:11:42,244 --> 00:11:46,827
environment, and it enables side-by-side
comparison of recommendations.

176
00:11:46,925 --> 00:11:49,887
So you have recommendations coming
from the old model and the new

177
00:11:49,891 --> 00:11:55,590
model, and then you can see, the
improvements or any, any gaps that

178
00:11:55,597 --> 00:11:59,074
are not visible in offline testing.

179
00:11:59,559 --> 00:12:03,241
Running shadow mode gives a safer
place to discover these issues.

180
00:12:04,130 --> 00:12:09,650
Coming to, the sh- further building upon
the shadow mode, so what we can do is

181
00:12:09,788 --> 00:12:12,585
canary, canary releases in credit system.

182
00:12:13,918 --> 00:12:17,746
It is very common in software
engineering, but credit decisioning

183
00:12:17,747 --> 00:12:19,550
requires a broader definition of success.

184
00:12:20,117 --> 00:12:24,801
So in web applications or applications,
you generally look at things like

185
00:12:24,801 --> 00:12:29,930
load and latency, error rates,
utilization, CPU utilization or server

186
00:12:29,930 --> 00:12:32,466
utilization, and system availability.

187
00:12:32,768 --> 00:12:35,024
but these are not enough
for a credit model.

188
00:12:35,593 --> 00:12:39,242
a credit canary needs to monitor at
least three dam-- additional dimensions.

189
00:12:39,684 --> 00:12:43,351
First, obviously the model behavior
and decision edge distribution.

190
00:12:43,401 --> 00:12:46,656
The second being the credit exposure
and the fairness indicators,

191
00:12:47,233 --> 00:12:50,500
and then finally the customer
outcomes and portfolio signals.

192
00:12:51,262 --> 00:12:56,086
So ultimately, a credit model exists,
to make decisions that affect the

193
00:12:56,086 --> 00:13:00,586
real customers and also the f- the
portfolio of the financial institution.

194
00:13:01,187 --> 00:13:07,205
So we need to look into, all these three
additional, aspects, which are not common

195
00:13:07,221 --> 00:13:13,481
with any other software, applications such
as websites or similar kind of things.

196
00:13:14,775 --> 00:13:19,233
So before expanding exposure,
we need predefined expansion

197
00:13:19,235 --> 00:13:22,764
criteria, investigation triggers,
and rollback conditions.

198
00:13:23,315 --> 00:13:27,273
So a canary is not simply like,
okay, let it turn on for a few

199
00:13:27,275 --> 00:13:28,657
customers and see what happens.

200
00:13:29,439 --> 00:13:33,613
It's more of a governed canary
where we have already decided what

201
00:13:33,615 --> 00:13:37,777
acceptable behavior looks like, what
would trigger an investigation, and

202
00:13:37,777 --> 00:13:39,569
what, what would cause us to stop.

203
00:13:40,015 --> 00:13:44,311
So that's where, the kill switches and
emergency rollback comes into picture.

204
00:13:44,962 --> 00:13:50,884
So if you If you see a model behaving
unexpectedly, an authorized team should

205
00:13:50,884 --> 00:13:56,858
be able to activate or deactivate
it and restore an approved fallback

206
00:13:56,909 --> 00:13:58,961
without requiring a new release.

207
00:14:00,025 --> 00:14:03,926
So this is one of the strongest arguments
for separating deployment from activation.

208
00:14:04,428 --> 00:14:07,521
If the new model is already deployed,
but activation is controlled

209
00:14:07,527 --> 00:14:10,876
through configuration, we can
potentially disable that model

210
00:14:10,911 --> 00:14:12,482
through approved control mechanism.

211
00:14:12,963 --> 00:14:15,116
But there is an important
governance requirement.

212
00:14:15,166 --> 00:14:18,892
A kill switch, can't simply mean
like someone can turn it off.

213
00:14:19,334 --> 00:14:24,036
We need to define, some attributes
like who has the authority, what

214
00:14:24,036 --> 00:14:28,539
conditions justify using it, and
how is the action documented?

215
00:14:28,917 --> 00:14:33,340
then not only that, but also
like what investigation follows,

216
00:14:33,707 --> 00:14:35,804
what happens after the rollback.

217
00:14:36,393 --> 00:14:40,132
So there is a, critical dependency
here, which is fallback readiness.

218
00:14:40,181 --> 00:14:43,725
So the previous model or policy
needs to remain production-ready

219
00:14:43,734 --> 00:14:44,852
during the rollout period.

220
00:14:45,401 --> 00:14:46,868
That's the most important one.

221
00:14:46,882 --> 00:14:52,492
So you can't just, kill the existing,
production model and say, we

222
00:14:52,493 --> 00:14:53,900
don't have anything to fall back."

223
00:14:54,508 --> 00:14:59,880
We need to have a previous model or at
least a policy needs to remain, which

224
00:14:59,882 --> 00:15:04,970
might be more of like a BAU rule-based
thing, mostly for credit decisioning.

225
00:15:05,725 --> 00:15:11,217
So we need to have a fallback when,
when we are going for a kill switch.

226
00:15:12,532 --> 00:15:16,894
And, I mean, rollback strategy isn't
real if the fallback path has been

227
00:15:16,900 --> 00:15:19,096
removed or is still no longer functional.

228
00:15:20,051 --> 00:15:22,834
so it should be part of the
original deployment strategy.

229
00:15:24,645 --> 00:15:31,167
Then, this leads to another key
area, which is feature flags,

230
00:15:31,962 --> 00:15:35,258
and how we integrate feature
flags with model observability.

231
00:15:35,942 --> 00:15:40,795
So feature flags and observability
create a control loop.

232
00:15:41,352 --> 00:15:46,787
by that what I mean is so you are
looking at every flag decision

233
00:15:47,875 --> 00:15:49,971
and the data that is generated.

234
00:15:50,635 --> 00:15:52,760
So what is the version of the model?

235
00:15:52,925 --> 00:15:54,306
What is the policy path?

236
00:15:54,503 --> 00:15:55,879
What was the timestamp?

237
00:15:56,272 --> 00:15:58,541
What is the rollout cohort?

238
00:15:59,107 --> 00:16:02,703
all this, because, that will
gives us the most important

239
00:16:02,703 --> 00:16:04,128
aspect, which is the traceability.

240
00:16:05,072 --> 00:16:11,152
so it becomes, So you observe, you
identify the risk, then govern,

241
00:16:11,994 --> 00:16:15,430
then intervene, and again observe.

242
00:16:16,082 --> 00:16:21,069
So this is more powerful than treating
deployment like, okay, we got approval

243
00:16:21,114 --> 00:16:24,866
and then just implement and then go ahead.

244
00:16:25,866 --> 00:16:30,903
This, this brings up the most important
part for, the financial institutions.

245
00:16:30,980 --> 00:16:33,784
As you know, financial
institutions are-- financial

246
00:16:33,798 --> 00:16:35,780
institutions are heavily regulated.

247
00:16:36,323 --> 00:16:43,454
So, we need to take into consideration not
just the customer experience, in terms of

248
00:16:43,454 --> 00:16:51,586
like whether model is available all the
time, but also what the model decision is.

249
00:16:52,188 --> 00:16:52,768
Is it fair?

250
00:16:53,487 --> 00:16:57,500
Can we, can we look at the model
and say it's, it's treating

251
00:16:57,500 --> 00:16:59,597
everybody in a similar way?

252
00:17:01,085 --> 00:17:05,649
So that's where we need to look at,
look at the decision outcomes and

253
00:17:05,660 --> 00:17:07,575
look at the fairness aspect as well.

254
00:17:08,383 --> 00:17:10,406
so for that, we need audit trail.

255
00:17:10,486 --> 00:17:15,679
We need to know, which flag was,
active, which, which model version was

256
00:17:15,686 --> 00:17:23,161
associated with this decision, what was
the decision, what attributes, had most

257
00:17:23,161 --> 00:17:25,282
important value in making that decision.

258
00:17:25,845 --> 00:17:30,594
If, if, if say the decision is a
negative, then you need to explain,

259
00:17:30,681 --> 00:17:33,355
what are the adverse action reasons.

260
00:17:33,452 --> 00:17:37,035
and along with that, look into
the attributes that define

261
00:17:37,035 --> 00:17:39,286
those adverse action reasons.

262
00:17:39,748 --> 00:17:45,709
and importantly, you also need to
understand, okay, is this the customer the

263
00:17:45,710 --> 00:17:52,065
only person affected or a set of similar
customers are also affected by that?

264
00:17:52,125 --> 00:17:56,230
You are saying, "Okay, this is
a systemic issue, but not a, a

265
00:17:56,231 --> 00:17:58,496
single one-on-- one-off issue."

266
00:18:00,016 --> 00:18:05,978
So we need to understand the traceability
aspect, and we need to make sure that

267
00:18:06,076 --> 00:18:11,193
we are able to trace every decision
that we-- that the model is making.

268
00:18:12,703 --> 00:18:17,915
So then, then it, it, results in,
a, a bigger governance aspect.

269
00:18:18,471 --> 00:18:24,398
So, Technology itself doesn't solve
governance, so we, we need to understand

270
00:18:24,935 --> 00:18:32,158
w-who is the authority at different
levels, and also we need to have

271
00:18:32,158 --> 00:18:34,358
a clear defined approval workflow.

272
00:18:35,068 --> 00:18:38,549
Depending on the use case that it can
involve, like technical validation,

273
00:18:38,554 --> 00:18:43,564
model risk review, then you have the
business approvals, compliance approvals,

274
00:18:44,064 --> 00:18:47,470
and, and, and maybe other control
functions depending upon the institution.

275
00:18:48,352 --> 00:18:50,603
but approval is only one part.

276
00:18:50,703 --> 00:18:52,704
Every flag also needs an owner.

277
00:18:53,542 --> 00:18:56,619
Someone has to be responsible
for monitoring it, someone has to

278
00:18:56,619 --> 00:18:59,817
maintain its documentation, someone
has to review whether it is still

279
00:18:59,817 --> 00:19:03,820
needed, and someone has to remove
it when its purpose is complete.

280
00:19:04,447 --> 00:19:08,790
so that brings us, the most important
concept, which is expiration.

281
00:19:09,396 --> 00:19:13,033
Temporary rollout flags should
have de-defined life cycles.

282
00:19:13,553 --> 00:19:17,507
otherwise, organizations accumulate
permanent flags, and eventually no

283
00:19:17,507 --> 00:19:19,219
one remembers why a flag exists.

284
00:19:20,330 --> 00:19:25,649
So that creates configuration complexity,
and it creates a governance risk.

285
00:19:25,831 --> 00:19:29,612
So we should treat feature flags
almost like a governed asset.

286
00:19:30,303 --> 00:19:33,502
It should have a, it should
have an owner, it should have a

287
00:19:33,502 --> 00:19:37,992
purpose, it should have like, okay,
what are the approvals you need?

288
00:19:38,551 --> 00:19:43,270
Of course, it should ne-- it should
have a monitoring mechanism and, and

289
00:19:43,350 --> 00:19:50,123
definitely an end date So for this to
work at scale, it needs to be integrated

290
00:19:50,126 --> 00:19:52,627
into the platform architecture.

291
00:19:53,340 --> 00:20:00,828
The-- So here, we want to, discuss, this
architecture that, that, helps us, in,

292
00:20:00,839 --> 00:20:02,915
in overcoming implementation challenges.

293
00:20:03,777 --> 00:20:10,184
It should have a-- It, it should
have several components, including

294
00:20:10,202 --> 00:20:13,624
feature platform, model serving,
feature management, and of

295
00:20:13,624 --> 00:20:15,215
course, decision orchestration.

296
00:20:16,110 --> 00:20:21,179
As, as these are part of the same
integrated system, these components

297
00:20:21,180 --> 00:20:24,721
need to work together so that when
orchestration selects a model through

298
00:20:24,732 --> 00:20:28,208
a flag, the resulting decision
remains traceable through the

299
00:20:28,208 --> 00:20:33,550
model input features, policy logic,
and mo- and governance records.

300
00:20:34,155 --> 00:20:35,719
but there are also challenges.

301
00:20:35,871 --> 00:20:39,266
One is, flag proliferation.

302
00:20:39,766 --> 00:20:44,056
If every model change creates another
permanent flag, our system becomes

303
00:20:44,091 --> 00:20:45,741
extremely difficult to manage.

304
00:20:46,587 --> 00:20:50,774
And then another one equally
important is unclear ownership.

305
00:20:51,257 --> 00:20:54,183
A flag can outlive the
team that created it.

306
00:20:55,052 --> 00:20:59,585
And, one more aspect is the
configuration dependency.

307
00:20:59,969 --> 00:21:03,355
A model flag might depend on
feature flags, policy flags,

308
00:21:03,409 --> 00:21:06,035
or say infrastructure flags.

309
00:21:06,421 --> 00:21:10,289
changing one without understanding the
others can create unexpected states.

310
00:21:11,257 --> 00:21:13,907
And of course, this is,
this is my favorite one.

311
00:21:13,961 --> 00:21:15,419
finally, poor naming.

312
00:21:15,737 --> 00:21:19,526
An identifier that makes perfect
sense to an engineer may be completely

313
00:21:19,527 --> 00:21:25,958
opaque to, say, a risk analyst or a
business analyst or a governance person.

314
00:21:26,740 --> 00:21:30,245
So the platform needs standards,
when you are naming these flags.

315
00:21:30,340 --> 00:21:35,283
it should have naming standards, ownership
standards, approval standards, expiration

316
00:21:35,283 --> 00:21:37,686
standards, and documentation standards.

317
00:21:38,686 --> 00:21:44,524
At this point, so, I want to emphasize
something that feature flags are

318
00:21:44,563 --> 00:21:46,198
not the solutions by themselves.

319
00:21:46,953 --> 00:21:50,841
They, they provide a mechanism,
but organizations need the

320
00:21:50,842 --> 00:21:55,202
right operating culture that
mechanism-- around that mechanism.

321
00:21:55,845 --> 00:21:58,253
The presentation tries to capture this.

322
00:21:58,755 --> 00:22:01,515
So gradual activation
should be considered.

323
00:22:02,157 --> 00:22:04,941
unexpected results should
trigger investigation.

324
00:22:05,395 --> 00:22:09,191
So you need to understand
where the issue is coming from.

325
00:22:09,871 --> 00:22:15,042
if a decision is made, you need
to trace it all the way to the

326
00:22:15,120 --> 00:22:16,787
earlier steps in the workflow.

327
00:22:17,751 --> 00:22:22,877
And, if an issue comes and the decision
is made that you have to stop it,

328
00:22:23,367 --> 00:22:25,219
you need to have a rollback option.

329
00:22:25,795 --> 00:22:31,818
So it is a, it is a-- rollback is a,
a, a legitimate control action, but

330
00:22:31,856 --> 00:22:34,507
not as a failure, we have to think.

331
00:22:35,495 --> 00:22:38,326
So this is very important
in financial services.

332
00:22:38,851 --> 00:22:42,497
there can be significant pressure to
deliver a new model, improve performance,

333
00:22:42,603 --> 00:22:49,051
get better lift, meet the growth targets
or, or any other business target.

334
00:22:50,432 --> 00:22:53,559
But when a model influences
credit decisions, the safest

335
00:22:53,624 --> 00:22:56,516
answer is not always go faster.

336
00:22:57,584 --> 00:23:01,029
Sometimes the response-- the
responsible answer is, let's,

337
00:23:01,069 --> 00:23:03,140
let's observe a little longer.

338
00:23:03,384 --> 00:23:05,616
Let's investigate this segment.

339
00:23:05,772 --> 00:23:10,910
If, if need, you have to pause or
even like in worst case scenario,

340
00:23:10,919 --> 00:23:13,483
you have to roll back, to a
previous version of the model.

341
00:23:14,487 --> 00:23:19,746
So a mature organization, doesn't
view those, actions as obstacles.

342
00:23:20,113 --> 00:23:22,963
It views them as part of
responsible model deployment.

343
00:23:23,603 --> 00:23:27,203
And this is where an integrated,
machine learning ops and platform

344
00:23:27,204 --> 00:23:28,567
capability becomes important.

345
00:23:29,701 --> 00:23:33,861
a shared platform can standardize,
model registration, feature

346
00:23:33,861 --> 00:23:38,924
flag integration, observability,
approval workflows, and rollback.

347
00:23:39,497 --> 00:23:42,889
that allows governance to become
consistent across the models rather

348
00:23:42,921 --> 00:23:44,840
than being reinvented every time.

349
00:23:46,008 --> 00:23:51,923
So let me, let me draw to the
conclusion of this presentation.

350
00:23:52,216 --> 00:23:58,102
feature flags allow us to transform
ML deployment from a single high-risk

351
00:23:58,120 --> 00:24:02,988
transition into a controllable,
controlled and, observable process.

352
00:24:03,582 --> 00:24:09,315
Instead of thinking, deployment as like
develop, validate, deploy, and done, now

353
00:24:09,327 --> 00:24:15,244
we can-- we have to think of a deploy,
then we have an observation layer,

354
00:24:15,285 --> 00:24:17,978
then an expansion, and then governance.

355
00:24:18,672 --> 00:24:22,421
So the men-- model enters production
infrastructure, but initially

356
00:24:22,435 --> 00:24:27,145
remains inactive we observe it
in shadow mode, we evaluate its

357
00:24:27,151 --> 00:24:31,740
behavior, then we progressively,
progressively expand exposure.

358
00:24:31,868 --> 00:24:36,624
Start with a small segment, then
expand as you see favorable,

359
00:24:36,742 --> 00:24:39,944
performance in terms of model behavior.

360
00:24:41,502 --> 00:24:46,405
we also monitor, model behavior,
customer outcomes, risk, and fairness.

361
00:24:46,918 --> 00:24:51,997
So all, all, all these as you're
monitoring and, and, and, and

362
00:24:51,998 --> 00:24:56,312
those metrics are on expected
lines, then you expand exposure.

363
00:24:57,192 --> 00:25:04,398
And through the process, we have to and we
need to maintain audit trails, ownership,

364
00:25:04,877 --> 00:25:07,788
and most importantly, rollback capability.

365
00:25:09,383 --> 00:25:15,528
So, for me, it's, the most important
takeaway from my per-- from the experience

366
00:25:15,533 --> 00:25:17,208
that I have gained all throughout.

367
00:25:17,767 --> 00:25:20,478
a machine learning model should
not only be accurate, but

368
00:25:20,494 --> 00:25:22,037
also should be controllable.

369
00:25:22,943 --> 00:25:27,761
In credit decisioning, we need systems
that allows us to answer four questions.

370
00:25:28,693 --> 00:25:30,371
What model made the decision?

371
00:25:31,185 --> 00:25:34,087
What happened after we activated it?

372
00:25:35,134 --> 00:25:42,128
Can we trace the model decision to the,
the earliest step, the most earliest step?

373
00:25:43,121 --> 00:25:49,388
And then can we safe, safely stop it if
it necessary and, have a fallback option?

374
00:25:50,383 --> 00:25:55,071
So feature flags are one mechanism
that can help answer those questions.

375
00:25:55,652 --> 00:26:00,799
but the most important and the
bigger opportunity is architecture.

376
00:26:01,283 --> 00:26:05,788
Feature management, MLOps, model
observability, risk governance, and

377
00:26:05,788 --> 00:26:07,969
decision orchestration operate together.

378
00:26:08,637 --> 00:26:13,159
We can create decisioning platforms
that are not only intelligent but

379
00:26:13,161 --> 00:26:19,556
also controllable, traceable, and
resilient, and ultimately safer for

380
00:26:19,557 --> 00:26:22,004
customers and financial institutions.

381
00:26:22,680 --> 00:26:25,540
my professional experience in
credit risk and model governance

382
00:26:25,540 --> 00:26:27,237
has reinforced one lesson for me.

383
00:26:27,753 --> 00:26:31,317
the goal of responsible AI isn't
simply to build better models.

384
00:26:31,950 --> 00:26:35,090
It is to build better
systems around these models.

385
00:26:35,925 --> 00:26:40,235
Systems that allow us to innovate
while maintaining control, systems

386
00:26:40,407 --> 00:26:45,432
that allow us to move quickly while
still being to stop, and systems that

387
00:26:45,451 --> 00:26:50,429
allow us to use machine learning at a
scale without losing, without losing

388
00:26:50,549 --> 00:26:52,170
transparency and accountability.

389
00:26:52,734 --> 00:26:55,438
That is the role I believe
feature flags can play in next

390
00:26:55,439 --> 00:26:57,131
generation of credit decisioning.

391
00:26:58,081 --> 00:26:59,580
So, thank you for your time.

392
00:27:00,321 --> 00:27:02,592
safer ML, smarter credit.

393
00:27:03,344 --> 00:27:03,948
Thank you, everyone.

